## Count tuples and express all conditions

### What you will learn

Understand aggregate identity and conditional literal expansion.

### Choose the aggregate tuple deliberately

An aggregate is a set of tuples. Both parcels weigh two, but the one-field tuple contains only the value 2 once. Adding the parcel identifier keeps two distinct tuples, giving a total weight of four.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    weight(letter,2). weight(packet,2).
    distinct_weights(Sum) :- Sum = #sum { Weight : weight(Parcel,Weight) }.
    shipment_weight(Sum) :- Sum = #sum { Weight,Parcel : weight(Parcel,Weight) }.
    parcel_count(Count) :- Count = #count { Parcel : weight(Parcel,Weight) }.
    #show distinct_weights/1. #show shipment_weight/1. #show parcel_count/1.
    """,
    models: :all
  )

[%{shown: ["distinct_weights(2)", "parcel_count(2)", "shipment_weight(4)"]}] = result.models
Course.display(result)
```

### Require every member of a domain

The conditional body expands ok(Item) for every required(Item). Only red is ok, so complete has no support while blue is required. Hiding the input facts leaves a satisfiable model with empty shown output.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    required(red;blue). ok(red).
    complete :- ok(Item) : required(Item).
    #show complete/0.
    """,
    models: :all
  )

[%{shown: []}] = result.models
:satisfiable = result.status
Course.display(result)
```

### Try it yourself

Add ok(blue). to the conditional example. Then remove both required facts and compare.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

In both cases complete is derived. With an empty required domain, the conditional body has no instances to falsify it; this is vacuous truth.

</details>

## Count distinct tuples

Predict the tuple set of each `#count` aggregate before evaluating its comparison. Identical tuples count once even if several substitutions produce them. Tuple components and variable scope therefore matter.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2). a(2,1). a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                 b(2,2).         b(3,3).
  c(S) :- S = #count{ Item,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  d(S) :- S = #count{   Item : a(Item,Other) }.
  e(S) :- S = #count{   Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  #show c/1. #show d/1. #show e/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2).         a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                                 b(3,3).
  c(S) :- S = #count{ Item,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  d(S) :- S = #count{   Item : a(Item,Other) }.
  e(S) :- S = #count{   Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  #show c/1. #show d/1. #show e/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2). a(2,1). a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                 b(2,2).         b(3,3).
  c(S) :- S = #count{   Item,Other : a(Item,Other);      Item,Other : b(Item,Other) }.
  d(S) :- S = #count{   Item,Other : a(Item,Other);      Third,W : b(Third,W) }.
  e(S) :- S = #count{ Item,Other,1 : a(Item,Other);    Item,Other,2 : b(Item,Other) }.
  f(S) :- S = #count{   Item,Other : a(Item,Other);    Item,Other,2 : b(Item,Other) }.
  #show c/1. #show d/1. #show e/1. #show f/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2).         a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                                 b(3,3).
  c(S) :- S = #count{   Item,Other : a(Item,Other);      Item,Other : b(Item,Other) }.
  d(S) :- S = #count{   Item,Other : a(Item,Other);      Third,W : b(Third,W) }.
  e(S) :- S = #count{ Item,Other,1 : a(Item,Other);    Item,Other,2 : b(Item,Other) }.
  f(S) :- S = #count{   Item,Other : a(Item,Other);    Item,Other,2 : b(Item,Other) }.
  #show c/1. #show d/1. #show e/1. #show f/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2). a(2,1). a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                 b(2,2).         b(3,3).
  c(1).           c(2).
  d(Item,S) :- c(Item), S = #count{   Other : a(Item,Other);        Third : b(Item,Third) }.
  e(Item,S) :- c(Item), S = #count{ Other,a : a(Item,Other);      Third,b : b(Item,Third) }.
  #show d/2. #show e/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2).         a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                                 b(3,3).
  c(1).             c(2).
  d(Item,S) :- c(Item), S = #count{   Other : a(Item,Other);        Third : b(Item,Third) }.
  e(Item,S) :- c(Item), S = #count{ Other,a : a(Item,Other);      Third,b : b(Item,Third) }.
  #show d/2. #show e/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  { a(Item) : n(Item) }.
  :- 2 #count{ Item : a(Item) }.
  #show a/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..3).
  { a(Item) : n(Item) }.
  :- 2 #count{ Item : a(Item) }.
  #show a/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) }.
  :- 2 #count{ Item,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  #show a/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) }.
  :- 2 #count{ Item : a(Item,Other) }.
  #show a/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) }.
  :- 2 #count{ Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  #show a/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  1 { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  1 { b(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  :- 2 #count{ Item,Other : a(Item,Other);   Third,W : b(Third,W) }.
  #show a/2. #show b/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  1 { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  1 { b(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  :- 2 #count{ Item,Other,a : a(Item,Other);   Third,W,b : b(Third,W) }.
  #show a/2. #show b/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  1 { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  1 { b(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  ok(Item) :- 2 #count{ Other : a(Item,Other);   Third : b(Item,Third) }, n(Item).
  :- not ok(1), not ok(2).
  #show a/2. #show b/2. #show ok/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  1 { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  1 { b(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  ok(Item) :- 2 #count{ Other,first : a(Item,Other);   Third,second : b(Item,Third) }, n(Item).
  :- not ok(1), not ok(2).
  #show a/2. #show b/2. #show ok/1.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Sum weighted tuple sets

For `#sum`, the first tuple component is the contribution. Distinct aggregate tuples contribute separately, while identical tuples are merged. Check both the tuple set and the resulting total.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2). a(2,1). a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                 b(2,2).         b(3,3).
  c(S) :- S = #sum{ 1,Item,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  d(S) :- S = #sum{   2,Item : a(Item,Other) }.
  e(S) :- S = #sum{  -1,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  f(S) :- S = #sum{   Item,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  #show c/1. #show d/1. #show e/1. #show f/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2).         a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                                 b(3,3).
  c(S) :- S = #sum{ 1,Item,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  d(S) :- S = #sum{   2,Item : a(Item,Other) }.
  e(S) :- S = #sum{  -1,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  f(S) :- S = #sum{   Item,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  #show c/1. #show d/1. #show e/1. #show f/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2). a(2,1). a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                 b(2,2).         b(3,3).
  c(S) :- S = #sum{ 1,Item,Other : a(Item,Other);   1,Third,W : b(Third,W) }.
  d(S) :- S = #sum{ 1,Item,Other : a(Item,Other);   2,Item,Other : b(Item,Other) }.
  #show c/1. #show d/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2).         a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                                 b(3,3).
  c(S) :- S = #sum{ 1,Item,Other : a(Item,Other);   1,Third,W : b(Third,W) }.
  d(S) :- S = #sum{ 1,Item,Other : a(Item,Other);   2,Item,Other : b(Item,Other) }.
  #show c/1. #show d/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2). a(2,1). a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                 b(2,2).         b(3,3).
  c(1).           c(2).
  d(Item,S) :- c(Item), S = #sum{ 1,Other : a(Item,Other);     1,Third : b(Item,Third) }.
  e(Item,S) :- c(Item), S = #sum{ 1,Other : a(Item,Other);     5,Other : b(Item,Other) }.
  #show d/2. #show e/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1,1). a(1,2).         a(2,2). a(2,3).
  b(1,1).                                 b(3,3).
  c(1).           c(2).
  d(Item,S) :- c(Item), S = #sum{ 1,Other : a(Item,Other);     1,Third : b(Item,Third) }.
  e(Item,S) :- c(Item), S = #sum{ 1,Other : a(Item,Other);     5,Other : b(Item,Other) }.
  #show d/2. #show e/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  { a(Item) : n(Item) }.
  :- 2 #sum{ Item : a(Item) }.
  #show a/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2). n(0).
  { a(Item) : n(Item) }.
  :- 2 #sum{ Item : a(Item) }.
  #show a/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) }.
  :- 2 #sum{ 1,Item,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  #show a/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) }.
  :- 2 #sum{ 1,Item : a(Item,Other) }.
  #show a/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) }.
  :- 2 #sum{ 1,Other : a(Item,Other) }.
  #show a/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  1 { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  1 { b(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  :- 4 #sum{ 2,Item,Other : a(Item,Other);   2,Third,W : b(Third,W) }.
  #show a/2. #show b/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  1 { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  1 { b(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  :- 3 #sum{ 1,Item,Other : a(Item,Other);   2,Third,W : b(Third,W) }.
  #show a/2. #show b/2.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  1 { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  1 { b(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  ok(Item) :- 2 #sum{ 1,Other : a(Item,Other);   1,Third : b(Item,Third) }, n(Item).
  :- not ok(1), not ok(2).
  #show a/2. #show b/2. #show ok/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  n(1..2).
  1 { a(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  1 { b(Item,Other) : n(Item), n(Other) } 1.
  ok(Item) :- 2 #count{ 1,Other : a(Item,Other);   2,Third : b(Item,Third) }, n(Item).
  :- not ok(1), not ok(2).
  #show a/2. #show b/2. #show ok/1.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Expand conditional literals

The conditions after a colon determine which literal instances participate. Track local bindings separately from variables shared with the outer rule. Compare the expanded instances with the computed model.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..2).
  c :- a(Item) : b(Item).
  #show c/0.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..3).
  b(1..3).
  c :- a(Item) : b(Item).
  #show c/0.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1).
  b(1..2).
  c :- a(Item) : b(Item).
  #show c/0.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..3).
  c :- a(Item) : b(Item).
  #show c/0.
  """,
  ~S"""
  b(1).
  c :- a(Item) : b(Item).
  #show c/0.
  """,
  ~S"""
  b(1..2).
  c :- a(Item) : b(Item).
  #show c/0.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..2).
  c(1..2).
  d :- a(Item) : b(Item), c(Item).
  #show d/0.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..2).
  c(1..3).
  d :- a(Item) : b(Item), c(Item).
  #show d/0.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..3).
  c(1..3).
  d :- a(Item) : b(Item), c(Item).
  #show d/0.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..2).
  c(1..2).
  d(Item) :- a(Item) : b(Item);   c(Item).
  #show d/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..2).
  c(1..3).
  d(Item) :- a(Item) : b(Item);   c(Item).
  #show d/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..3).
  c(1..3).
  d(Item) :- a(Item) : b(Item);   c(Item).
  #show d/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..2,1..2).
  c(1..2).
  d(Item) :- a(Other) : b(Item,Other);   c(Item).
  #show d/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..2,1..2). b(2,3).
  c(1..2).
  d(Item) :- a(Other) : b(Item,Other);   c(Item).
  #show d/1.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a(1..2).
  b(1..2,1..2). b(2,3).
  c(1..2). c(3).
  d(Item) :- a(Other) : b(Item,Other);  c(Item).
  #show d/1.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Apply aggregates to course selection

Extend the timetable example from [domains and grounding](04-domains-and-grounding.livemd#develop-a-course-selection-model). This section creates its own input files so you can run it independently. Contrast counts of courses, lecture tuples and conflicting pairs; then deliberately test aggregate safety.

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace()
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "facts.lp", ~S"""
credits( asp,9).
credits(  ml,9).
credits(ling,6).
credits(phil,9).
lecture(asp,thu,3). lecture(asp,fri,3).
lecture(ml,wed,2). lecture(ml,thu,3). lecture(ml,fri,4).
lecture(ling,tue,1). lecture(ling,thu,2).
lecture(phil,tue,3). lecture(phil,wed,4).
""")
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "enroll.lp", ~S"""
enroll(asp). enroll(ml). enroll(ling).
""")
```

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "basic_1.lp", ~S"""
course(C) :- lecture(C,D,P).
   day(D) :- lecture(C,D,P).
period(P) :- lecture(C,D,P).
slot(D,P) :- lecture(C,D,P).
""")
```

### Count selected atoms

Cardinality expressions constrain how many conditional literals hold. The threshold can come from a constant or an input fact. Counting enrolled courses differs from counting individual lecture slots: repeated lectures must not accidentally multiply a course count.

```elixir
~S"""
many_courses :- enroll(C1), enroll(C2), C1 != C2.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp")])
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
~S"""
many_courses :- enroll(C1), enroll(C2), C1 != C2,
                enroll(C3), C1 != C3, C2 != C3.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp")])
|> Course.display()
```

> **Note.** This example deliberately contains unsafe variables or an unfinished ellipsis. The cell displays the expected native diagnostic instead of stopping notebook evaluation.

```elixir
Course.expect_error(fn ->
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    many_courses :- enroll(C1), enroll(C2), C1 != C2, ...
    """,
    files: [Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp")],
    arguments: ["-c", "m=2"]
  )
end)
```

```elixir
~S"""
many_courses :- m { enroll(C) }.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp")], arguments: ["-c", "m=2"])
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
~S"""
many(m).
many_courses :- many(M), M { enroll(C) }.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp")], arguments: ["-c", "m=2"])
|> Course.display()
```

> **Note.** Use basic_1.lp, the domain file created earlier in this lesson.

```elixir
~S"""
many(m).
enroll(C,D) :- enroll(C), lecture(C,D,P).
many_courses(D) :- day(D), many(M), M { enroll(C,D) }.
#show many_courses/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(
  files: [
    Path.join(workdir, "facts.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "basic_1.lp")
  ],
  arguments: ["-c", "m=2"]
)
|> Course.display()
```

> **Note.** Use basic_1.lp, the domain file created earlier in this lesson.

```elixir
~S"""
many(m).
many_courses(D) :- day(D), many(M), M { enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P) }.
#show many_courses/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(
  files: [
    Path.join(workdir, "facts.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "basic_1.lp")
  ],
  arguments: ["-c", "m=2"]
)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
~S"""
#show enroll/1.
#show lecture/3.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "facts.lp"), Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp")])
|> Course.display()
```

#### Choose the counted object

The literal before a colon determines what is counted; the condition determines which instances participate. Local variables range inside the aggregate element. Global variables connect the aggregate to the rest of its rule.

#### Compare cardinalities

Lower and upper bounds can also be written with comparison operators. Identify the same counted set in each spelling before deciding whether the conditions are equivalent.

> **Note.** Use basic_1.lp, the domain file created earlier in this lesson.

```elixir
~S"""
many(m).
many_courses(D) :- day(D), many(M), M { enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P) }.
some_courses(D) :- day(D), 1 { enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P) } 2.
#show many_courses/1.
#show some_courses/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(
  files: [
    Path.join(workdir, "facts.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "basic_1.lp")
  ],
  arguments: ["-c", "m=2"]
)
|> Course.display()
```

#### Count lectures instead of courses

Put the lecture literal, including its slot, before the colon when separate lectures must contribute separately. Counting only the enrolled course would merge its repeated appearances.

> **Note.** Use basic_1.lp, the domain file created earlier in this lesson.

```elixir
~S"""
many(m).
many_lectures(D) :- day(D), many(M), M { lecture(C,D,P) : enroll(C) }.
lecture(ml,wed,1).
#show many_lectures/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(
  files: [
    Path.join(workdir, "facts.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "basic_1.lp")
  ],
  arguments: ["-c", "m=2"]
)
|> Course.display()
```

#### Keep variable scopes distinct

Renaming a variable local to an aggregate element is harmless when done consistently in that element. Renaming a shared outer variable only inside the aggregate changes the condition. Compare all three rules rather than assuming they agree.

```elixir
~S"""
day(mon;tue;wed;thu;fri).
many(m).
enroll(asp). lecture(asp,mon,1).
enroll(ml).  lecture(ml,mon,1). lecture(ml,mon,4).
extreme(D) :- day(D), many(M), M { enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P), P <= 1 ;
                                   enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P), P >= 4 }.
#show extreme/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(arguments: ["-c", "m=2"])
|> Course.display()
```

#### Check aggregate safety

Global variables need suitable outer bindings; variables local to an aggregate element need suitable bindings inside that element. The next program deliberately violates these requirements.

> **Note.** This example deliberately contains unsafe variables or an unfinished ellipsis. The cell displays the expected native diagnostic instead of stopping notebook evaluation.

```elixir
Course.expect_error(fn ->
  Clingo.solve!(~S"""
  extreme(D) :- day(D), many(M), M { enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P), P <= 1 ;
                                     enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P), P >= 4 }.
  extreme(D) :- day(D), many(M), M { enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P), P <= 1 ;
                                     not enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P), P >= 4 }.
  extreme(D) :- not day(D), many(M), M { enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P), P <= 1 ;
                                         enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P), P >= 4 }.
  extreme(D) :- day(D), many(M), M { enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P), P <= 1 ;
                                     enroll(C) : not lecture(C,D,P), P >= 4 }.
  """)
end)
```

### Count tuples

`#count` counts distinct tuples satisfying its conditions. Include enough tuple components to distinguish the objects you intend to count. Omitting a component can merge two contributions; including a fixed global value does not distinguish additional tuples.

> **Note.** Use basic_1.lp, the domain file created earlier in this lesson.

```elixir
~S"""
many(m).
many_courses(D) :- day(D), many(M), M { enroll(C) : lecture(C,D,P) }.
#show many_courses/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(
  files: [
    Path.join(workdir, "facts.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "basic_1.lp")
  ],
  arguments: ["-c", "m=2"]
)
|> Course.display()
```

#### Distinguish lecture tuples

The following aggregates count different tuple representations. A day bound outside the aggregate is fixed for that instance; a lecture’s course and period distinguish the contributions.

> **Note.** Use basic_1.lp, the domain file created earlier in this lesson.

```elixir
~S"""
many(m).
many_lectures(D) :- day(D), many(M), M { lecture(C,D,P) : enroll(C) }.
lecture(ml,wed,1).
#show many_lectures/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(
  files: [
    Path.join(workdir, "facts.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "basic_1.lp")
  ],
  arguments: ["-c", "m=2"]
)
|> Course.display()
```

#### Aggregate overlapping lectures

The next example counts lecture combinations at a slot. Inspect which tuple components identify a distinct conflict, and avoid counting one symmetric pair twice.

> **Note.** Use basic_1.lp, the domain file created earlier in this lesson.

```elixir
~S"""
many(m).
conflict(D,P) :- enroll(C1), lecture(C1,D,P),
                 enroll(C2), lecture(C2,D,P), C1 < C2.
many_conflicts( courses) :- many(M), M #count {     C : conflict(D,P), lecture(C,D,P) }.
many_conflicts(    days) :- many(M), M #count {     D : conflict(D,P), lecture(C,D,P) }.
many_conflicts(   slots) :- many(M), M #count {   D,P : conflict(D,P), lecture(C,D,P) }.
many_conflicts(lectures) :- many(M), M #count { C,D,P : conflict(D,P), lecture(C,D,P) }.
#show many_conflicts/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(
  files: [
    Path.join(workdir, "facts.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "enroll.lp"),
    Path.join(workdir, "basic_1.lp")
  ],
  arguments: ["-c", "m=2"]
)
|> Course.display()
```

#### Apply the same scope rules

The safety example now uses count aggregates. Its error is expected. Compare these scope rules with the sum and conditional examples above; [optimization and projection](06-optimization-and-projection.livemd) uses the resulting distinctions when ranking answers.

> **Note.** This example deliberately contains unsafe variables or an unfinished ellipsis. The cell displays the expected native diagnostic instead of stopping notebook evaluation.

```elixir
Course.expect_error(fn ->
  Clingo.solve!(~S"""
  extreme(D) :- day(D), many(M), M #count { C : enroll(C), lecture(C,D,P), P <= 1 ;
                                            C : enroll(C), lecture(C,D,P), P >= 4 }.
  extreme(D) :- day(D), many(M), M #count { C : enroll(C), lecture(C,D,P), P <= 1 ;
                                            C : not enroll(C), lecture(C,D,P), P >= 4 }.
  extreme(D) :- not day(D), many(M), M #count { C : enroll(C), lecture(C,D,P), P <= 1 ;
                                                C : enroll(C), lecture(C,D,P), P >= 4 }.
  extreme(D) :- day(D), many(M), M #count { C : enroll(C), lecture(C,D,P), P <= 1 ;
                                            C : enroll(C), not lecture(C,D,P), P >= 4 }.
  """)
end)
```

